    "narrative": "The engine room of Ghost Ride the WHIP for today\u2019s slate is undoubtedly our outfield trio. **James Wood** (76.28) leads the charge with an elite 0.437 xwOBA projection against J.T. Ginn, supported by a favorable park factor and a top-of-the-order assignment. **Mickey Moniak** (75.98) follows closely, benefiting significantly from a 14% platoon edge against Lowder and a prime #2 spot in the order. Meanwhile, **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92) is our strongest internal utility play; his ability to leverage a 14% platoon advantage against Canning, coupled with the SP\u2019s high 4.57 xERA, makes him a lock for production. These three players represent our highest upside for driving R and RBI totals while maintaining high batting average floors.\n\nRegarding bench decisions, the most difficult call was excluding **Austin Riley** (52.17). Despite his pedigree, **Riley** fell just outside the top-tier efficiency bracket due to a lackluster #7 lineup placement and a difficult matchup against Gore. He lost his spot to the aggregate output of our lower-slot options who held superior situational leverage. Risks today are concentrated in the infield: **Jose Caballero** (44.65) and **Zach McKinstry** (49.62) are both hovering near our marginal threshold due to unfavorable platoon splits and unfavorable #9 order assignments. While **Edwin Arroyo** (58.15) looks strong thanks to a massive 19% SP skill advantage against Sugano, he and our other infielders must remain disciplined. We are also keeping a close watch on **Corbin Carroll** (69.43); while his speed is vital for our scarcest category, his efficiency is slightly capped by an elite hold-runner catcher behind the plate, meaning he must be opportunistic on the basepaths to justify his high start-score.",
